      },
      // 32B DiT + ~24B Mistral TE. In-app Q4 *load* of the dense bf16 snapshot peaks
      // ~105 GB (> the 128 GB ceiling), so the snapshot is pre-quantized to Q4 ON DISK
      // by the install-time convert job (mlx.requiresConversion + the `flux2_dev_quant`
      // converter, sc-5917): the offline quantize peaks ~34.5 GB. The worker then loads
      // the packed dir (load `.quantize()` is a no-op on already-packed weights). Q4
      // weights floor ~30 GB but generate is activation-bound — 1024²/28 peaks ~74 GB
      // (sc-2365) — so minMemoryGb 96 (128 GB Macs only). The convert pre-quantizes
      // transformer + text_encoder and symlinks the (identical-to-klein) VAE +
      // tokenizer + model_index.json from the gated source snapshot.
      // Worker-layer footprint (Q4, 1024², /usr/bin/time -l peak): T2I ~74 GB, single-reference
      // edit ~81 GB — both within 96. Multi-reference edit (sc-6211): with the engine's
      // sequence-gated activation chunking (sc-6266) a 2-image edit peaks ~81 GB and a 4-image
      // ~93 GB (down from ~104 GB un-chunked, sc-5923) — so the canonical multi-reference edit is
      // now a first-class sub-96 GB mode, reachable via the Image-Studio multi-image picker
      // (ui.multiReference). minMemoryGb stays 96 (inflating it would gate the common 74 GB T2I
      // path behind 128 GB-only). The edit is still activation-bound, so the FLUX.2 provider's
      // calibrated safety contract (re-anchored on the chunked curve) rejects the genuinely
      // over-budget combinations (≥3 references / higher resolution that exceed the machine's
      // unified memory) with an actionable message instead of a silent OOM.
