        }
      },
      // candle/CUDA VRAM gate — q4/q8/bf16 all MEASURED (sc-13174, completing the sc-12631 deferral after
      // the epic sc-12732 rework landed the 24 GB path). Taken on an idle RTX PRO 6000 (Blackwell sm_120) via
      // the candle-gen-wan VramProbe at the A14B's shipped 1280x720 / 81f / 4-step Lightning default, in the
      // SEQUENTIAL-offload residency the model now renders with: the two 14B MoE experts are swapped
      // one-resident-at-a-time (sc-12733), the bf16 UMT5 TE (sc-12778) is offloaded before denoise, and the
      // z16 bf16 VAE decode is tiled against free VRAM (sc-12734/12758/12818) — so at most ONE expert +
      // activations are live. The 24 GB lever was sc-12894 (8x-finer sdpa chunk; the denoise attention was
      // the floor). q4/q8/bf16 are the driver's TRUE concurrent-live peak (`CU_MEMPOOL_ATTR_USED_MEM_HIGH`,
      // sc-12818) in GiB — the honest figure; nvidia-smi reads the cudarc pool high-water (~27/37/49 GB, base-10
      // q4/q8/bf16, inflated — the pool never frees) and under-samples the attention transient. Each measured in
      // its own process. The peak is DENOISE-owned; the VAE decode adds only ~5 GiB.
      //
      // This model loads SEQUENTIAL, not resident: both bf16 experts co-resident is ~56 GiB + TE + VAE and
      // OOMs even a 96 GB card (the pre-rework pathology sc-12730 fixed), so there is no meaningful
      // resident peak — `video_load_spec` forces `OffloadPolicy::Sequential` for the A14B ids
      // (`video_jobs::candle_wan_offload_policy`) and these are that SEQUENTIAL peak. The gate is only
      // truthful because the load is coupled to it (pinned by the gpu_and_manifest tripwire). minMemoryGb
      // 24 gates the DEFAULT/lightest (q4) tier (22.13 + the gate's 2 GB headroom), which admits a 32 GB
      // RTX 5090 — the epic sc-12732 target. (The raw 22 GiB q4 peak physically fits a 24 GB card too, but
      // the conservative headroom targets ~26 GB free, so a 24 GB card is refused — the safe direction.)
      //
      // q8 (27.95) is gated at its LIVE peak, which is what admits a 32 GB card (sc-13174). Its live peak is
      // 27.95 GiB but its nvidia-smi POOL high-water is 36.9 GB (34.4 GiB) and cudarc never frees the pool, so
      // whether a ≤32 GB card packs down to the live peak IS the question. sc-13174 argued yes from a 64 GiB
      // balloon leaving ~31 GiB free: a valid clip, the SAME 27.95 GiB live peak, comparable wall time
      // (~2388 s vs the unconstrained 2427 s), and balloon(64) + live(28) = 92 < 96 GiB.
      //
      // ⚠️ THAT ARGUMENT DOES NOT HOLD, and the tier has NOT been re-measured (sc-16091 → sc-16118). Both
      // premises fail:
      //   1. The arithmetic is CIRCULAR. `balloon(64) + live(28) = 92 < 96` uses the LIVE figure, which is the
      //      correct figure only IF the pool trimmed — the very claim under test. Under the competing
      //      hypothesis it reads balloon(64) + high-water(34.4) = 98.4 > 95.6, a ~2.8 GiB overcommit.
      //   2. "A WDDM spill would inflate the wall time" is MEASURED FALSE on this host (sc-15791, 2026-07-30):
      //      a 3.41 GiB pool-reserved footprint ran with only 1.93 GiB driver-visible free — a 1.48 GiB
      //      overcommit — completing with a bit-identical peak at 1.07x, and FASTER in a sibling run. The
      //      driver absorbs the overage silently. Note sc-13174's own ballooned run was faster than
      //      unconstrained (2388 vs 2427 s), which under the old rule read as proof of no spill.
      // Both hypotheses predict everything observed, because USED_MEM_HIGH reports LIVE bytes and so reads
      // 27.95 either way. If the pool does NOT trim, q8's real footprint on a 32 GB card is ~34.4 GiB and this
      // gate OVER-ADMITS — a user OOMs on a card this manifest says fits. It may well be fine (pool trimming is
      // documented driver behaviour); it is simply not established. The value is LEFT UNCHANGED deliberately:
      // correcting it needs the measurement, not a comment edit. sc-16118 settles it under an ENFORCED ceiling
      // (`CUmemPoolProps.maxSize` + `cuDeviceSetMemPool`, proven in inference #335 to bind candle's allocator)
      // instead of a balloon, which is not a ceiling on this hardware.
      //   bf16 (38.56) is MEASURED (sc-13174): its dense fp32 `Wan-AI/*-Diffusers` snapshot was staged (the
      // missing transformer_2 + other fp32 shards downloaded) and run — one dense bf16 expert (~26 GiB,
      // 14B×2 B) + the shared ~12 GiB activation set. It replaces the old conservative DERIVED 56 bound: the
      // real 38.56 GiB live peak admits a 48 GB card the 56 bound refused, while staying refused on a 32 GB
      // card (40.56 > 32). Its pool high-water (48.8 GB ≈ 45.4 GiB) sits ABOVE the live peak like q8's does,
      // and no separate ≤48 GB balloon was run — so ⚠️ bf16-on-48 INHERITS the unproven q8 pool-trim inference
      // above in full, with no independent evidence of its own. Same treatment: value unchanged, sc-16118.
      "candle": {
        "minMemoryGb": 24,
        "vramGbByTier": { "q4": 22.13, "q8": 27.95, "bf16": 38.56 },
        "vramMeasuredPixels": 1048576,
        "measured": true
      },
